# cd-review — Run the one AI review through its four validators (BLUEPRINT §9).
#   Doctrine §3.3: zero authority over headline numbers. Its role holds no INSERT or UPDATE
#   on any dial or envelope number, so the limit is a grant and not an instruction.
#
# ==========================================================================================
# WRITTEN, NOT BUILT. RT-B IS FORBIDDEN IN WO-2.
# ==========================================================================================
# PBG-RT-010 keeps image creation and container startup as separate, separately authorised
# actions, and its verification note states that CONTAINER_READY_DESIGN together with
# CONTAINER_BUILD_NOT_YET_AUTHORISED is a complete and valid state. This file is the design.
# Building it, smoke-testing it and observing the runtime UID on a RUNNING container are
# WO-3 work, recorded as owed and not as done.
#
# ==========================================================================================
# THE BASE IMAGE IS A BUILD ARGUMENT WITH NO DEFAULT, AND THAT IS THE POINT
# ==========================================================================================
# "A tag is a mutable pointer; a digest is an identity" (Standards Baseline §3). A tag-only
# base reference must fail the register check, so there is no tag here to fail on.
#
# The Python base image is not present on this host and resolving its digest would require
# `docker pull`, which is outside this seat's runtime authorisation. Writing a
# plausible-looking digest nobody verified would be constructing evidence, which is
# non-waivable. So the base arrives as an argument with NO DEFAULT: `docker build` refuses a
# blank base name, and `docker compose` refuses the unset variable before that. Both fail
# closed, and nothing is claimed that was not observed.
#
# The required value, and the record of what has and has not been verified, is in
# deploy/base-images.json.
ARG CD_PYTHON_BASE
FROM ${CD_PYTHON_BASE}

# SINGLE STAGE, DELIBERATELY. The containerisation skill asks for multi-stage so build
# tooling stays out of the runtime image — and this application has NO build step at all.
# It runs on the interpreter with PYTHONPATH=src and no install (BLUEPRINT §11, A1), so
# there is no compiler, no wheel build and no package manager invocation to isolate. A
# build stage here would exist only to be empty.

LABEL org.opencontainers.image.title="cd-review"
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.description="Run the one AI review through its four validators. Zero authority over headline numbers (BLUEPRINT SS9)."
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.vendor="crash-detector"
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.licenses="UNLICENSED"
# Set at build time from the exact commit being built (Standards Baseline §3: refer to
# things by identity). Declared with no default so an unidentified image cannot be built.
ARG CD_BUILD_SHA
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.revision="${CD_BUILD_SHA}"

# NON-ROOT, DECLARED EXPLICITLY AND NUMERICALLY (PBG-RT-012).
# Numeric because some orchestrators enforce runAsNonRoot by inspecting the UID and cannot
# resolve a name. A distinct UID per service, so a compromised container's identity is not
# every container's identity.
RUN groupadd --system --gid 10006 cd-review \
 && useradd --system --uid 10006 --gid 10006 --no-create-home --shell /usr/sbin/nologin cd-review

WORKDIR /app

# READ-ONLY CODE — the first of the three storage classes (PBG-RT-013).
# Copied owned by root and NOT by the runtime user, so the process cannot modify its own
# code even before `read_only: true` is applied by compose. Ephemeral scratch is the tmpfs
# at /tmp; durable data belongs to the store's named volume alone, and this image mounts it
# nowhere.
COPY --chown=root:root --chmod=0555 src/ /app/src/
COPY --chown=root:root --chmod=0555 deploy/entrypoints/ /app/entrypoints/

# .dockerignore, OWED AND STATED. The build context is the repository root, so a
# `.dockerignore` must exist there before this is built, excluding at least: .git/,
# __pycache__/, .pytest_cache/, .env*, *.pem, *.key, plants/, governance/ and pbg/.
# Without it the build context carries the repository history — and the governance record —
# into the daemon. Creating a file at the repository root is outside this increment's
# writable scope, so it is recorded as owed for WO-3 rather than taken quietly.

ENV PYTHONPATH=/app/src \
    PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 \
    PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1 \
    CD_CONTRACTS_DIR=/contracts

# contracts/ is mounted READ-ONLY at /contracts by compose, not baked in: the envelope
# contracts are shared by several services and are read through $CD_CONTRACTS_DIR
# (crash_detector.publication.envelope_contract), so a container may mount them anywhere
# without a code change.
#
# NO `VOLUME ["/contracts"]`, AND THE ABSENCE IS DELIBERATE (PBG-RT-013).
#
# An earlier draft of this file declared one. It is wrong, in a way that is worth stating
# because it reads as correct: `VOLUME` declares the DURABLE storage class, and /contracts
# is in the READ-ONLY CODE class. Three consequences followed from the confusion.
#
#   1. Run outside compose, `VOLUME` makes Docker create an ANONYMOUS volume at /contracts
#      — writable, undeclared, and surviving the container. BLUEPRINT §2 says exactly one
#      named volume exists and it is the store's; this quietly created six more.
#   2. It contradicts the read-only bind compose mounts at the same path, so the artefact
#      and the compose file would disagree about which class the path belongs to.
#   3. `VOLUME` freezes the path's contents at declaration and discards later image
#      writes, which is a silent failure mode for a path whose whole job is to be current.
#
# The read-only bind in deploy/compose.yaml is the mechanism, and it is sufficient. A test
# asserts no Dockerfile here declares a VOLUME at all.


USER 10006:10006

# SIGTERM means shut down cleanly within the grace period. The handling is in
# crash_detector.runtime.lifecycle — in the APPLICATION, because a process that ignores
# SIGTERM cannot be fixed from outside it.
STOPSIGNAL SIGTERM

# EXEC FORM, NOT SHELL FORM. Shell form makes /bin/sh PID 1, and the shell does not forward
# SIGTERM to its child — the process would be killed rather than asked, every time.
# `-B` keeps the interpreter from writing bytecode into a read-only code directory.
ENTRYPOINT ["python3", "-B", "/app/entrypoints/cd_review.py"]
